on is asking for r as a percent. So, 0.0617 is 6.17%, so r is 6.17, as we've found. So, yeah, that should be the answer.\n\nJust to recap:\n\n- Given: 6% annual interest, compounded monthly.\n\n- To find: equivalent annual rate (EAR) if compounded once a year.\n\n- Formula: EAR = (1 + (r/n))^n - 1.\n\n- Plug in numbers: (1 + 0.06/12)^12 - 1 \u2248 1.0617 - 1 = 0.0617.\n\n- Convert to percentage: 6.17%.\n\nTherefore, r is approximately 6.17 percent.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe equivalent annual interest rate is \\boxed{6.17}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the equivalent annual interest rate \\( r \\) that would give the same return as a 6% annual interest rate compounded monthly, we need to calculate the effective annual rate (EAR).\n\nThe formula for the effective annual rate is:\n\\[ \\text{EAR} = \\left(1 + \\frac{r}{n}\\right)^n - 1 \\]\n\nGiven:\n- The annual interest rate \\( r = 6\\% = 0.06 \\)\n- The compounding period per year \\( n = 12 \\)\n\nFirst, compute the inside of the left side:\n\\[ 1 + \\frac{0.06}{12} = 1 + 0.005 = 1.005 \\]\n\nNext, raise this to the power of 12:\n\\[ (1.005)^{12} \\]\n\nUsing the approximation:\n\\[ (1.005)^{12} \\approx 1.0617 \\]\n\nThus, the effective annual rate is:\n\\[ 1.0617 - 1 = 0.0617 \\]\n\nConvert this to a percentage:\n\\[ 0.0617 \\times 100 = 6.17\\% \\]\n\nTherefore, the equivalent annual interest rate \\( r \\) is approximately 6.17%.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{6.17}\n\\]",
