tep-by-step is more accurate because I multiplied each time, but perhaps I made an error in that process.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I can use another approach. Since I know that (1 + 0.06/12)^12 is equal to e^{12 * ln(1 + 0.06/12)}.\n\nSo, let's compute it more precisely.\n\nFirst, compute ln(1.005). Let me compute ln(1.005) more accurately.\n\nWe know that ln(1.005) \u2248 0.004979, but let me compute it more accurately.\n\nUsing the Taylor series expansion for ln(x) around x=1:\n\nln(1 + x) \u2248 x - x^2/2 + x^3/3 - x^4/4 + ...\n\nFor x=0.005,\n\nln(1.005) \u2248 0.005 - (0.005)^2 / 2 + (0.005)^3 / 3 - (0.005)^4 / 4 + ...\n\nCompute each term:\n\n0.005 = 0.005\n\n(0.005)^2 / 2 = 0.000025 / 2 = 0.0000125\n\n(0.005)^3 / 3 = 0.000000125 / 3 \u2248 0.0000000416667\n\n(0.005)^4 / 4 = 0.00000000015625 / 4 \u2248 0.0000000000390625\n\nSo, ln(1.005) \u2248 0.005 - 0.0000125 + 0.0000000416667 - 0.0000000000390625 \u2248\n\nCompute 0.005 - 0.0000125 = 0.0049875\n\n0.0049875 + 0.0000000416667 \u2248 0.0049875416667\n\n0.0049875416667 - 0.0000000000390625 \u2248 0.0049875416266\n\nSo, ln(1.005) \u2248 0.0049875416266\n\nTherefore, 12 * ln(1.005) \u2248 12 * 0.0049875416266 \u2248\n\nCompute 10 * 0.0049875416266 = 0.049875416266\n\nCompute 2 * 0.0049875416266 = 0.0099750832532\n\nAdd together: 0.049875416266 + 0.0099750832532 \u2248 0.059850499519\n\nSo, exponentiate e^{0.059850499519}\n\nCompute e^{0.059850499519}\n\nAgain, using Taylor series for e^x around x=0:\n\ne^x \u2248 1 + x + x^2/2 + x^3/6 + x^4/24 + ...\n\nx = 0.059850499519\n\nCompute each term:\n\n1 = 1\n\nx \u2248 0.059850499519\n\nx^2 / 2 \u2248 (0.059850499519)^2 / 2 \u2248 0.003585 / 2 \u2248 0.0017925\n\nx^3 / 6 \u2248 (0.059850499519)^3 / 6 \u2248 (0.000215) / 6 \u2248 0.0000358333\n\nx^4 / 24 \u2248 (0.059850499519)^4 / 24 \u2248 (0.0000128) / 24 \u2248 0.0000005333\n\nSo, adding them up:\n\n1 + 0.059850499519 \u2248 1.0598504995\n\n1.0598504995 + 0.0017925 \u2248 1.061643\n\n1.061643 + 0.0000358333 \u2248 1.061678833\n\n1.061678833 + 0.0000005333 \u2248 1.061679366\n\nSo, e^{0.059850499519} \u2248 1.061679366\n\nTherefore, (1.005)^12 \u2248 1.061679366, so 1 + r \u2248 1.061679366, so r \u2248 0.061679366, which is approximately 6.1679366%, which is about 6.17% when rounded to the nearest hundredth.\n\nSo, that seems consistent with my step-by-step calculation.\n\nWait, so in the natural logarithm method, I get 1.061679366, which is approximately 1.06168, so 6.168%, which is 6.17% when rounded.\n\nEarlier, when I used the approximation e^{0.059748} \u2248 1.06156864, I think that might have been an over-approximation because I used e^{0.06} as 1.06183654 and adjusted it down by 0.000252, but perhaps the exact value is higher.\n\nWait, but in any case, using the more accurate calculation via the Taylor series, we have (1.005)^12 \u2248 1.061679366, so r \u2248 6.1679366%, which is approximately 6.17%.\n\nTherefore, the equivalent annual interest rate is 6.17%.\n\nWait, so I think that 6.17% is the correct answer.\n\nBut just to make sure, let's see what the exact value is.\n\nUsing the formula for the effective annual rate: EAR = (1 + r/n)^n - 1.\n\nGiven r = 0.06, n = 12,\n\nEAR = (1 + 0.06/12)^12 - 1\n\nCompute 0.06 / 12 = 0.005\n\n1 + 0.005 = 1.005\n\n1.005^12 \u2248 ?\n\nAlternatively, if I use a calculator, 1.005^12 \u2248 1.061678, so EAR \u2248 0.061678, which is 6.1678%, which is 6.17% when rounded to the nearest hundredth.\n\nTherefore, the answer is 6.17%.\n\nWait, but in my first calculation, I thought it was 6.07%, but that was a miscalculation because I didn't carry out the accurate step-by-step.\n\nSo, in conclusion, the equivalent annual interest rate r is approximately 6.17%.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe equivalent annual interest rate is \\boxed{6.17\\%}.\n</think>\n\nDr. Fu Manchu has a bank account with an annual interest rate of 6 percent, compounded monthly. We need to find the equivalent annual interest rate \\( r \\) that would give the same return if compounded annually.\n\nFirst, we use the compound interest formula for monthly compounding:\n\\[ A_{\\text{monthly}} = P \\left(1 + \\frac{0.06}{12}\\right)^{12} \\]\n\nWe set this equal to the amount after one year with annual compounding:\n\\[ A_{\\text{annual}} = P (1 + r) \\]\n\nSince both amounts are equal for one year, we have:\n\\[ \\left(1 + \\frac{0.06}{12}\\right)^{12} = 1 + r \\]\n\nSimplifying the inside of the left side:\n\\[ 1 + \\frac{0.06}{12} = 1 + 0.005 = 1.005 \\]\n\nThus, the equation becomes:\n\\[ (1.005)^{12} = 1 + r \\]\n\nWe compute \\( (1.005)^{12} \\) using logarithms and the exponential function:\n\\[ \\ln(1.005) \\approx 0.004979 \\]\n\\[ 12 \\times 0.004979 \\approx 0.059748 \\]\n\\[ e^{0.059748} \\approx 1.061679 \\]\n\nTherefore:\n\\[ (1.005)^{12} \\approx 1.061679 \\]\n\\[ 1 + r \\approx 1.061679 \\]\n\\[ r \\approx 0.061679 \\]\n\nRounding to the nearest hundredth, we get:\n\\[ r \\approx 6.17\\% \\]\n\nThe equivalent annual interest rate is \\boxed{6.17\\%}.",
